"""Central editorial content for the ASL static website. Avoids unsourced claims."""
SITE={'name':'Aurum Standard Laboratories','short':'ASL','caption':'Assay of Minerals, Gold, Diamonds & Gemstones','tagline':'Every valuable material deserves a closer look.'}
SERVICES=[
 {'slug':'mineral-assay','title':'Mineral assay','short':'Minerals','eyebrow':'01 / MINERALS','image':'mineral-assay','lead':'Mineral assay begins with a representative question.','intro':'Mineral specimens can vary significantly within a deposit, shipment or hand sample. Before analytical numbers are useful, it is important to understand where material came from and how the sample represents the material of interest.','summary':'Consider mineral composition, sampling and the measurements that matter to your decision.','sections':[('What might be present?','Analytical work may seek to identify major constituents, target metals, impurities or other characteristics relevant to a commercial or technical question. The appropriate measurement depends on the material, the elements of interest and the intended decision.'),('Why sampling matters','Composition can change between locations or even between particles in the same batch. Sample collection, preparation, splitting and documentation therefore affect how an analytical result should be understood. Precisely measuring an unrepresentative specimen can still lead to a misleading conclusion about a larger lot.'),('Making findings useful','Results should describe the specimen assessed, the measurements and relevant units. Consider the method, reporting limits and whether the selected sample can reasonably represent the material of wider interest.')], 'prepare':['Material description and sample form','Source or collection location, if known','Approximate quantity or number of samples','Previous analyses or available documentation','Target elements or questions of interest'], 'faq':[('Can one small sample represent an entire stockpile?','Not automatically. Representativeness depends on the material’s variability and how the sample was taken.'),('Can an assay establish the value of a deposit?','Composition is one input. Resource estimation and commercial feasibility require further evidence.')], 'related':['gold-assay','sample-handling']},
 {'slug':'gold-assay','title':'Gold assay','short':'Gold','eyebrow':'02 / GOLD','image':'gold-assay','lead':'Understanding gold starts with the right measurement.','intro':'Appearance or weight alone does not establish the composition of a gold-bearing material. A useful enquiry starts with the form of the specimen and the particular question: content, fineness, composition or suitability for a next step.','summary':'Understand gold content, fineness and the relevance of representative samples.','sections':[('Different forms, different requirements','Jewellery, bars, grains, doré, dust and mineral materials present different sampling and analytical challenges. A method appropriate to one form may not offer equivalent information for another.'),('Fineness and composition','Fineness describes the proportion of gold within a material. Its meaning depends on how the material was selected, measured and reported. A sample result should not automatically be treated as a finding about an entire lot.'),('From examination to interpretation','The intended use of a result shapes the question and suitable approach. Preparation, other constituents and technical limitations all affect what a measurement can establish.')], 'prepare':['Material form: jewellery, grains, bar, dust or ore','Approximate weight or quantity','Known source and any prior result','Whether the specimen is representative of a larger lot','The specific information required'], 'faq':[('Is colour a reliable measure of purity?','No. Appearance alone cannot establish gold content.'),('Does testing one item certify an entire shipment?','Not without appropriate representative sampling and verification.')], 'related':['mineral-assay','reporting']},
 {'slug':'diamonds','title':'Diamond examination','short':'Diamonds','eyebrow':'03 / DIAMONDS','image':'diamond-examination','lead':'Examine the details that shape diamond understanding.','intro':'Diamond examination brings together material identification and observations relevant to description and evaluation. The right approach begins with understanding exactly what the question asks.','summary':'Explore observable characteristics, identification and responsible interpretation.','sections':[('Identity and material context','An initial enquiry may concern whether a specimen is diamond, what properties can be observed and which additional tests or existing documents are relevant. Identifying a material is different from establishing its geographic origin.'),('Characteristics that matter','Cut, clarity-related features, colour, measurements and condition may matter, depending on whether the specimen is rough or polished and what information is sought.'),('Results in context','An examination should describe the item and observations actually made. Formal grading, treatment detection and distinction between natural and laboratory-grown material require appropriate methods and competence. Visual inspection alone does not answer every question.')], 'prepare':['Rough or polished condition','Number of pieces and approximate dimensions','Photographs and existing documents, if available','Whether the stone is loose or mounted','The particular assessment question'], 'faq':[('Can a photograph establish diamond identity?','Photographs can support an initial discussion but do not replace suitable physical examination.'),('Does identification establish origin?','No. Identification and geographic provenance are separate questions requiring different evidence.')], 'related':['gemstones','reporting']},
 {'slug':'gemstones','title':'Gemstone examination','short':'Gemstones','eyebrow':'04 / GEMSTONES','image':'gemstone-examination','lead':'See gemstones beyond their appearance.','intro':'Gemstones differ in composition, crystal structure, inclusions and possible treatment. Understanding a specimen requires distinguishing the identity question from wider questions about appearance, characteristics and history.','summary':'Examine gemstone identity, observable characteristics and treatment-related questions.','sections':[('Start with identification','Colour alone is rarely sufficient to identify a stone. Careful assessment draws on suitable observations, known material properties and, where relevant, analytical methods.'),('Treatment and origin questions','Questions about natural versus treated material, synthesis and geographic origin are distinct. Not every stone or technique allows every question to be resolved conclusively.'),('Documenting what is seen','A useful written description identifies the specimen and separates direct observations from further interpretations. That distinction helps people understand what a finding actually supports.')], 'prepare':['Suspected stone type, if known','Cut or rough condition and piece count','Approximate dimensions or weight','Existing documents and photographs','Your question about identity or characteristics'], 'faq':[('Does colour prove a gemstone’s identity?','No. Different mineral species or other materials can show similar colours.'),('Is the origin always determinable?','No. Geographic-origin attribution depends on suitable evidence and may remain indeterminate.')], 'related':['diamonds','sample-handling']},
 {'slug':'sample-handling','title':'Sample reception & handling','short':'Sample handling','eyebrow':'05 / RECEPTION','image':'sample-handling','lead':'Every reliable result has a starting point.','intro':'Before examination, a specimen needs a clear identity and suitable accompanying record. Good reception, handling and documentation connect subsequent findings to the right material.','summary':'Understand specimen identification, packaging, intake and chain-of-custody principles.','sections':[('A considered reception','Useful information includes sender details, sample description, piece count or quantity, distinguishing marks, declared material and the purpose of the enquiry. Packaging should protect contents and support safe handling.'),('Maintaining identity','Consistent identifiers and movement records reduce the risk of confusion between specimens. Valuable, fragile, unusual or hazardous materials may require special advance arrangements.'),('A clear release','Result delivery and physical release or return of an item should follow agreed identification and authorisation checks. The handling approach should fit the material and the service scope.')], 'prepare':['Contact details and specimen descriptions','Count, approximate mass or distinguishing marks','Known handling risks and packaging needs','Accompanying reports or documents','Return instructions or special requirements'], 'faq':[('Should I send the material straight away?','Discuss acceptance, packaging and transport arrangements before sending valuable or potentially hazardous material.'),('Why record identifiers?','They link each observation or result back to the correct physical specimen.')], 'related':['reporting','mineral-assay']},
 {'slug':'reporting','title':'Results & reporting','short':'Reporting','eyebrow':'06 / REPORTING','image':'reporting','lead':'Information becomes valuable when it is clear.','intro':'A report has value when the reader can identify the examined material, understand what was measured or observed and recognise the limits and context of the result.','summary':'Make sense of specimen references, findings, units and reporting scope.','sections':[('Know what was tested','A well-structured result names the item or sample reference and describes the relevant examination context. It avoids implying findings beyond specimens actually assessed.'),('Explain findings appropriately','Numeric results require appropriate units; descriptive findings need clear terms. Direct observations should be separated from interpretation and important limitations should be identified.'),('Keep documentation organised','Traceable records help link findings to the right item and enable later questions. A technical report is not automatically a regulatory certificate or an independent accreditation endorsement.')], 'prepare':['Purpose of the result','Correct specimen reference or description','Intended recipient or decision context','Any specification for comparison','Any question about reporting units'], 'faq':[('Is an assay report the same as a certificate?','Not necessarily. The meaning and authority of a document depend on its scope, issuer and any applicable regulatory framework.'),('Can results be applied to an untested lot?','Only if the sampling approach and evidence support that interpretation.')], 'related':['sample-handling','gold-assay']},
]
