PILLARS=[('Identity','Know precisely which material a result concerns.'),('Suitability','Match the question and examination approach.'),('Traceability','Keep relevant observations and references connected.'),('Interpretation','Explain only what the evidence supports.'),('Confidentiality','Treat specimen and enquiry information with care.'),('Responsibility','Make expectations and limitations clear.')]
ARTICLES=[
 {'slug':'understanding-gold-assay','title':'What does a gold assay actually tell you?','category':'Gold & precious metals','image':'gold-assay','minutes':5,'excerpt':'Explore what content, fineness and sample relevance mean in practical terms.','related':'gold-assay','sections':[
 ('The question behind the measurement',["Gold may appear as an ingot, jewellery, grains, dust or a component within mineral-bearing material. These forms differ in ways that influence sampling and interpretation. An assay begins by defining what is being measured and why the result is needed.","A weight on a scale tells you how much material is present, not the proportion that is gold. Appearance is similarly insufficient: alloying metals, surface coatings and heterogeneous particles can all affect what an item looks like without establishing its overall composition."]),
 ('Understanding fineness',["Fineness expresses the proportion of gold within the tested material. It may be described in parts per thousand, mass percent or another recognised convention. The units must be clear to avoid confusing concentration with total mass.","The laboratory method and specimen preparation matter. Surface observations may not establish the composition of the interior; a sub-sample of a mixed lot may not capture the variability of the whole. The result belongs to the specimen that was actually examined."]),
 ('Using the result responsibly',["A meaningful report connects an identifier to the measured material, records suitable method context and presents the finding in interpretable units. Comparing results between different testing approaches requires understanding their scope and limitations.","If a commercial decision concerns a large lot, a representative sampling strategy can be as important as the analytical method. Ask how the sample was selected before assuming that a single result describes all material in the shipment."])]},
 {'slug':'why-mineral-sampling-matters','title':'Mineral sampling: why the specimen matters','category':'Minerals & sampling','image':'mineral-assay','minutes':6,'excerpt':'How variability, collection and preparation shape the meaning of an analysis.','related':'mineral-assay','sections':[
 ('Material is rarely uniform',["A mineral-bearing body may change over short distances. Even a small pile of crushed material can contain particles with sharply different compositions. What is observed in one hand specimen cannot automatically be extended to every part of the source.","A useful assay question therefore starts with the target population: a single rock, a sample bag, a stockpile or material from a defined collection interval. Different targets call for different sampling approaches."]),
 ('Representative does not mean simply large',["Increasing sample mass can help in some circumstances, but size is not the only consideration. The number and distribution of collection points, particle size, mixing and splitting can each affect the sample’s usefulness.","Preparation should preserve the relationship between the material received and the test portion ultimately examined. Correct identification through crushing, mixing and sub-sampling reduces avoidable ambiguity."]),
 ('Reading a mineral result',["A concentration value is linked to the test portion and relevant measurement method. Units, reporting limits and the suite of elements examined influence what can be concluded.","A result may be technically precise while still being a poor description of the intended batch if the original sample was biased. When applying data to exploration or commerce, analytical evidence should be paired with a defensible sampling plan."])]},
 {'slug':'diamonds-and-gemstones-examination','title':'Diamonds and gemstones: identity versus evaluation','category':'Precious stones','image':'gemstone-examination','minutes':6,'excerpt':'Why identity, observable characteristics and origin require different evidence.','related':'gemstones','sections':[
 ('Begin by naming the question',["An owner might ask whether a transparent stone is diamond, whether a coloured stone is a particular mineral species, or whether characteristics affect its description. Those questions can overlap, but they are not interchangeable.","The information available from visual observation depends on the item, lighting, magnification and its condition. Features seen with the eye may suggest further tests but do not automatically provide a definitive identification."]),
 ('Identity and characterisation',["Gemological identification may involve combinations of optical, physical and other observations. Descriptive features such as colour, inclusions, form and condition can matter, yet their significance depends on the material.","Diamond grading considerations may refer to cut, clarity, colour and size. An informal observation is not a substitute for a formal grading exercise with clearly stated scope and applicable standards."]),
 ('Treatment and provenance',["The question of natural versus laboratory-grown material differs from a geographic-origin question. Treatment detection may require specialised methods, and some origins cannot be established with confidence from a single observation.","A careful report makes clear which item was examined, what was observed and which interpretations are reasonably supported. It is appropriate for some questions to remain unresolved rather than overstate certainty."])]},
 {'slug':'reading-laboratory-reports','title':'How to read a laboratory report with confidence','category':'Reports & interpretation','image':'reporting','minutes':5,'excerpt':'Understand identifiers, reported units, observations and the scope of findings.','related':'reporting','sections':[
 ('Find the specimen identity first',["A report should be read against the exact sample or item it describes. Check identifiers, piece counts, material form and any relevant dates or references before interpreting a number or conclusion.","Where there are several samples, matching each result to the correct specimen is essential. A result for one stone or test portion does not automatically describe other materials supplied at the same time."]),
 ('Read units and context together',["Percent, parts per million, mass fractions and fineness conventions describe different quantities. Confirm which unit applies and whether values describe a measured concentration, an observation or an interpreted classification.","Methods have intended applications and limitations. Technical notes, reporting thresholds, sample preparation details and uncertainty information—when supplied—give context that a single result alone does not convey."]),
 ('Avoid conclusions beyond the report',["A reported property is not automatically a statement of market value, regulatory compliance or geological provenance. Those wider conclusions may require other records, methods or professional assessments.","When a term is unclear, ask which specimen and method it refers to and what can reasonably be inferred. A sound report supports informed questions as well as informed decisions."])]}
]
ARTICLE_BY_SLUG={a['slug']:a for a in ARTICLES}
